{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZNkUqxHrXaiXem239Uu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate to the `candidate_3` refinement slot, and it passes the self-check with `OK`. One problem, though: its PSIS-LOO estimate may be flagged as unreliable at admission. If that happens, the model can be admitted but not exported as the winner.\n\n**Refined model:** `local_representativeness`, the incumbent.\n\n**Name:** `individual_alternation_prototype`\n\n**The one change:** In the incumbent, everyone shares a single prototype alternation rate: the switching rate a sequence has to be close to before it looks random. Here each participant has their own rate, drawn from a population distribution. It is fitted as a non-centred hierarchy on the logit scale, bounded between 0.05 and 0.95. Everything else is unchanged: the multiscale local-balance term, the periodic-template penalty and the choice rule. I only swapped the flat priors for smoother Beta, LogNormal and Normal ones.\n\n**Why:** The critique's one discrepancy that survives the FDR is that people differ far more in how often they pick the more-alternating sequence than the incumbent predicts (0.217 observed vs 0.053, q = 0.016). A per-person prototype rate is the most direct fix for that within the same mechanism.\n\n**Self-check:**\n- **ELPD-LOO:** −1051.5 on the 100-draw smoke fit, against the incumbent's −1398.4. That is a very large gain, but the comparison is rough because the smoke fit is a single short chain.\n- **Pareto k:** 8.5% of trials (218 of 2560) have k > 0.5, well above the 1% tolerance. A longer fit may bring this down, but per-participant parameters often keep k high.\n- **Convergence:** not verified, because a single chain can't report R-hat.\n\n**Other things to know:**\n- The model binds `participant_id`, so the novelty gate averages it over the training participants. It will also be screened out of EIG designs, which is recorded in `screened_out.json`.\n- The model reserves room for up to 512 participant IDs (`MAX_PARTICIPANTS = 512`). Participants it has never seen stay at the population prior, so held-out participants and later experiments won't crash it until IDs pass that limit."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3476,"cache_read_input_tokens":53552,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3476,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"308c0c73-1c3a-4cf5-b447-c7fa28d2ca15","uuid":"35ba006e-c9ad-4c24-89e2-76c85b252791","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:19:50.180Z","request_id":"req_011CfZNkUKSyuFDk21LME7KY"}
